WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.540
YUFENG GUO: One of the top
things that I get asked about

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.100
is AutoML.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.960
In this two-part
video, let's try

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.520
using AutoML Vision to build and
deploy a machine learning model

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.850
that recognizes
different types of chairs

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.430
along with a few other items
sprinkled in for good measure.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.440
We're doing it all--

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.020
from the raw data
collection, all the way

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.130
to serving the model, and
everything in between.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.050
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.780
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:32.850
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.760
And on this episode, we'll
explore AutoML Vision,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.650
currently in alpha,
and get our data

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.020
into the right
format as an input.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.680
In part 2, we'll use this
data to build a model

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.410
to detect what style of
chair is in a picture.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.000
One of the things that
makes AutoML so compelling

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.460
is having a custom model.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.370
Existing models and services,
like the Vision API,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.910
have no problem recognizing
that these pictures have

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.540
chairs in them.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.990
But what if you made
your own chairs,

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.730
and that you needed
a way to catalog

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:09.780
the various brands of different
chairs in your inventory?

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.440
Wouldn't it be nice to be
able to use a custom Vision

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.200
API, so to speak,
which recognizes

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.820
your particular chairs?

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.340
That's what we're going to
try to do with AutoML Vision.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.250
AutoML Vision takes as
an input lots and lots

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.960
of labeled photos.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.560
So get your camera out and
start snapping pictures.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.000
How many pictures you ask?

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.340
Well, ideally,
you'd have hundreds

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.330
of pictures per object.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.990
And that would be good.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.700
And then, once you get tired of
clicking that shutter option,

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.250
perhaps you'll try
doing what I did to get

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.380
lots and lots of pictures.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.960
To make it easier to
capture data for AutoML,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.080
I chose to gather
my training data

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.640
by capturing videos
of the chairs

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.190
and then using the tool ffmpeg
to extract out those frames.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.070
I've gone out over to the
Google Sunnyvale campus.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:07.030
I took some videos of a variety
of different outdoor chairs.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:11.160
I also took some video of the
tables they were positioned

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.380
around, as well as a bike-- eh--

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.030
just to make things a
little more interesting.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.150
Let's take a look at
some of that footage now.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.940
Here, we have some chairs
of different shapes, styles,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.230
and colors.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.320
No video is greater than
30 seconds in length.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.740
And we have a short clip of a
table and another of a bike.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.770
That's the data that we
will be working with.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.070
The end state that
we want is a CSV file

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:39.090
that has one row per
image and two columns--

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.910
the first for the location
of the image in Google Cloud

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.190
Storage, and the
second for the label,

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.670
like red chair or
table or blue chair.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.940
To make it easier to
organize, I placed each video

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.890
in its own folder.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.640
So then we can run ffmpeg
on each video file in turn

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.590
within those folders.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.560
That way, once the
frames are extracted out,

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.710
you'll have one folder
per label filled

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.770
with images of just that label.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.470
This is a handy way to
organize your images and data.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.630
And it's simpler than
having one massive folder

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.780
of all the images intermingled.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.900
Next, we can upload images
to Google Cloud Storage,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.570
using gsutil, replicating
our folder structure

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.560
of one folder per label.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.980
Now, it's time to get to
work creating a CSV file that

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.860
lists the path and label
for each image I wanted

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.440
to include in my data set.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.830
Now, there's lots of
ways to accomplish this.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:39.000
But I chose to spin up
a local Jupyter notebook

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.250
and create a Pandas
data-frame that

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.100
gets exported as a CSV file.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.350
Let's walk through
that code now.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.290
OK, so here we are in
our Jupyter notebook.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.290
And I've imported
OS and Pandas, so we

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.730
can work with the file system.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:58.000
And load up Pandas-- that way we
can use that to export our CSV.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.960
I've listed out all
the folders that I want

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.490
to use to pull in the data.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.310
And I've listed this
out here explicitly

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:08.100
because, perhaps in
a different scenario,

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.730
I might choose to use just
a subset of these folders

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.760
to create a different
training set.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.200
So then we have a
setup here to create

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.400
a dictionary of our
folder name and then

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.380
mapping to an array of all
of our files for that folder.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.950
And so to do that,
first, I'm going

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.690
to create an array of arrays
of just all those file names.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:37.740
Each inner array is all the
file names of one folder.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.580
And the outer array is
just for each folder.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:45.240
And so here, we're listing
through all the files

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.370
in a folder.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.120
So our data folder is here.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.490
And we'll wrap that in an array
so that we can capture that.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.250
And so we can see,
here, I've printed

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:58.441
a sample of one value, one
image, in each of the folders.

00:04:58.441 --> 00:05:00.190
And we can see that
they're all different.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.240
So it looks like
we're in good shape.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.960
And so we can take those two--

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.505
our folder names
and all our file

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:06.840
names-- and zip them together.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.460
Pass them to create
a dictionary.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:14.290
Using that and our base
Google Cloud Storage path,

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:16.040
we can glue these two
together, because we

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.660
want to pre-pen the GCS, or
Google Cloud Storage path,

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.856
in the final output.

00:05:22.856 --> 00:05:24.480
So what we want is
something like this,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.310
where we have the Google
Cloud Storage directory path,

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:31.769
all the way out to the .jpeg
name, and then a comma,

00:05:31.769 --> 00:05:33.060
and then the name of the label.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.700
So in this case,
maybe we have a comma

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:40.600
and then chair_black, like so.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:44.055
And so to do that, we'll loop
over the entire dictionary.

00:05:44.055 --> 00:05:48.450
And for each file in
the array of file names

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:52.030
in that dictionary,
we will put together

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:56.460
the base Google
Cloud Storage path,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:01.140
along with the folder
name and file name,

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.620
as well as that label.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:07.740
So now, what we have with us
here is an array of pairs.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.920
Each pair has that
path and the label.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.810
So we can take this
array and just pass it

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.280
straight into a data frame.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:18.570
Pandas accepts that
format to create new data

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.570
frames from scratch.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.740
And our data frame looks
exactly as we'd expect.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:24.880
We got all our paths
and all our labels.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.310
Now, we're ready
to export to CSV.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:30.770
In this particular case,
we want to export such

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:34.700
that we use index equals
false and header equals false.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.980
This is important and
can really trip you up

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:38.090
if you forget to do this.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.460
Because if you leave
the defaults, which

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.920
are index-- true,
and header-- true,

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.730
the outputted CSV file will
have a header row of 0 and 1.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.150
And moreover, it will
have an index column

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.030
that comes before everything
else, which is just going

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.032
to be your 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:06:57.032 --> 00:06:58.240
So we don't want any of that.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.560
We just want the
path to the file

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.260
and the label, nothing else.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.330
So we use index-- false,
and header-- false.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:10.020
So if you organize your folder
structure the same way that I

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.240
have mine, you can
take this notebook

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.370
and, with a couple
of minimal changes,

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.180
adapt it for your
use case as well.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.780
So I'll definitely publish
this notebook and link

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:23.550
to that down below in
the video description.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:26.550
All right, so now
we have a CSV file

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.730
describing the location and
label for all the images

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.320
in our data set.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.540
We're ready to train our model.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.190
Join me in part 2
of this episode.

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:40.680
And we'll see just how well
this model can perform.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.880
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

